>
  <p>
    Rewired, a Des Moines electrical company, went through upwards of a dozen marketing agencies
    before Local Service Rocket. Twice, bigger “best in class” firms took over with flashy website
    rebuilds. Organic and paid both tanked. One rebuild soaked tens of thousands of dollars and
    months of weaker lead flow. John came back each time — and stayed. The company went from
    roughly <strong>$60k to $220k+ average months</strong>, 5 to 12 crew, and about 1.5 to 6 booked
    calls a day over 3+ years.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you’re an electrician shopping for a
    <a href="/for/electrician-marketing">marketing partner</a> because the current one “isn’t big
    enough,” this is the story to read first.
  </p>

  <h2>What does a desperate first call with an electrical owner sound like?</h2>
  <p>
    John was LSR’s first client. He wasn’t shopping features. He was in a brutal stretch — running
    jobs himself after an office betrayal, years of paying marketers who never made advertising
    profitable — and he talked for the better part of an hour before the real ask landed:
    <em>want to give it a go?</em>
  </p>
  <p>
    The honest pitch was low budget, maximum effort, no fake certainty. He’d already tried five or
    six companies. One more experiment wasn’t optimism. It was necessity. Full arc on the
    <a href="/case-studies/rewired">Rewired case study</a>, including John’s
    <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1DnfD-5fWL8">video</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How do you make Google Ads work when the site is locked?</h2>
  <p>
    Early on we were blocked from changing the existing website. So we shipped a conversion-focused
    landing experience anyway and got
    <a href="/services/paid-ads">Google Ads</a> producing on a small daily budget — after multiple
    agencies had already failed at the same job.
  </p>
  <p>
    That matters for electrician marketing specifically. Electrical demand is high-intent and
    competitive. You do not need a six-month rebrand to prove ads can book work. You need a page
    that converts and an account someone actually owns.
  </p>

  <h2>What happens when you hire the “bigger” agency?</h2>
  <div class="receipt">
    <div class="receipt-label">THE REBUILD DETOURS</div>
    <p>
      Twice, larger agencies took over with flashy rebuilds. Organic and paid both tanked. One
      rebuild soaked tens of thousands of dollars and months of weaker lead flow before John pulled
      the work back.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    The hard part wasn’t that the new sites looked bad. It’s that they ignored the URLs, relevance,
    and ads hygiene that were already paying the bills. A brochure rebuild that kills organic is
    one of the most expensive mistakes an electrical company can buy — and it usually arrives
    wrapped as “best in class.”
  </p>
  <p>
    What kept the partnership alive was a simple rule: if someone else can do it better, they
    should. Alistair offered to step aside, stay on as a light consultant for context, and return
    only if the new team couldn’t deliver. They couldn’t. That humility, plus actually making ads
    print money, is why Rewired stopped cycling agencies.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    “My company went through upwards of a dozen other marketing companies prior to landing here,
    and I can say it’s nice to be home where you’re treated like family.”
  </blockquote>

  <h2>What does “own the whole stack” mean for an electrician?</h2>
  <p>
    Eventually Rewired stopped shopping. We improved the site for conversion (not just looks), kept
    ads performing, ran
    <a href="/services/local-seo">local SEO</a>, and later
    <a href="/services/hiring">Facebook hiring campaigns</a> for helpers, apprentices, and
    salespeople when demand outran the crew.
  </p>
  <p>
    The outcome that matters isn’t a lead chart. It’s the business numbers:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Average monthly revenue: about $60k → $220k+</li>
    <li>Crew: 5 → 12</li>
    <li>Booked calls per day: about 1.5 → 6</li>
    <li>Still a client after 3+ years</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That’s what electrician marketing is supposed to look like when someone is accountable for the
    whole account — not a specialist team where fixing the boring foundation is nobody’s job. Same
    pattern that burned
    <a href="/blog/google-category-relevance">Motha Electric</a> on a single wrong GBP category.
  </p>

  <h2>What should electrical owners take from this?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Bigger agency ≠ better outcome. Ask for recent booked-job proof in your trade.</li>
    <li>Never hand over a site rebuild without a plan for existing URLs and rankings.</li>
    <li>Prove ads on a small budget before you fund a six-figure “transformation.”</li>
    <li>Keep ownership of your Google profile, ads account, and website.</li>
    <li>
      When demand is healthy, the next bottleneck is usually
      <a href="/blog/realistic-marketing-expectations">people</a>, not another pitch deck.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you’re comparing partners right now, read
    <a href="/blog/bad-vs-good-agency">bad vs good home-service agencies</a> next — then decide
    whether you want a slideshow or a calendar that fills.
  </p>
